-- title:
--   Bijectivity of θ on label-(g,j) points at algebraically closed fields
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld frame for fake elliptic curves, with the following data.
--
--   *Arithmetic coefficients.* Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a non-zero natural number $N$ which is squarefree and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`). A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$, with $\mathcal{O}$ complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and $(r) = (\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. A characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal{O}$ with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). A monoid homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet} : GL_2(K_0) \to \mathbb{Z}$ (written multiplicatively) with $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some $u \in \mathcal{O}^{\times}$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   *Indefinite quaternion side and moduli.* Rationals $a,b$ such that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfies `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`, i.e. $a>0$ or $b>0$ and, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$, the completion at $v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`), containing every rational integer (`hΛℤ`). A scheme $\mathcal{X}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ with a point map `pt` for fake elliptic curves with level-$N$ structure, satisfying `IsCoarseModuli` (`h𝒳`: invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, surjectivity and injectivity on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). An auxiliary level $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N)=1$, a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ with point map `ptF` for curves with full level $n$ satisfying `IsFineModuli` (`hM`), a group $G$ acting on $M$ through $\rho$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying `IsLevelTwistAction` (`hρ`: the automorphisms lie over the base, the twist by $\chi(g)$ of a full-level structure is carried to the translate of its point by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative and bijective modulo $n$). A forgetful morphism $p : M \to \mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$, invariant under $\rho$ and compatible with the point maps (`hp`, `hρp`, `hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, a scheme $\mathcal{Y}_\ell$ over $\mathcal{O}$ with point map for curves with extra level $\ell$ satisfying `IsCoarseModuliT` (`h𝒴`), together with two degeneracy maps $d_0,d_1 : \mathcal{Y}_\ell \to \mathcal{X}$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on points send an extra-level datum to its underlying curve, respectively to any quotient related to it by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (`hd₀`, `hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal{X}$ over the base realising on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *Definite quaternion side.* Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ ramified exactly at $\bar r$ (`hdef`); a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hΛ₁`, `hR₁`, `hRΛ₁`); a finite-adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) such that `meetOrder R₁ n₁` is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map $\iota_0$ of the definite algebra into $M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ containing $r$. A subgroup $\Gamma^t$ of the unit group whose elements are exactly those of `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v` (`hΓt`), elements $s_\ell$ and finite-adelic units $sf_\ell$ for each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ with (`hs`) the component of $sf_\ell$ at each place not containing $r$ equal to $s_\ell \otimes 1$, its component at the place above $r$ equal to $1$, the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lying in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$; the groups $\Gamma^t_\ell = \Gamma^t \cap s_\ell\Gamma^t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); and a unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma^t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   *Base objects over the residue field and the endomorphism dictionary.* A coordinate map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}^{W} \times \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}^{W}$ (values in `Zp2 r`) satisfying `IsOrderCoord` (`hcoord`: additive, unital, multiplicative for the Frobenius-twisted product, injective, with dense image modulo every power of $r$, and trace-compatible). Over $\bar k = Onr/(\pi)$: a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ with level-$N$ structure, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the zero section of $A_0$, and `hA₀ : A₀.IsFormalModuleVia coord X₀ θ₀`, identifying the formal group of $A_0$ with that of $X_0$ compatibly with the $\Lambda$-action through $\mathrm{coord}$. Families $e, e' : \Gamma^t \to \operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base with degrees $\deg$, subject to: `hE1` (each pair $(e_\gamma, e'_\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg \gamma}$ preserving the level structure), `hE1mul` (multiplicativity up to multiplication by powers of $r$), `hE1sc` (a scalar $c \in \Gamma^t$ gives multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$), `hE1'` (conversely, if $e_\gamma$ agrees up to an $r$-power with multiplication by a positive integer then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar), `hE2` (for every injective ring map $E_0'$ of the centraliser of the $\mathcal{O}_D$-action and of $\varpi$ on $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in GL_2(K_0)$ such that every $\gamma$ admits a centraliser element $\varepsilon$ inducing $e_\gamma$ on the formal coordinates on all nilpotent test algebras, with $E_0'(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$), `hE3` (for every full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ there are labels $\mathrm{lab} : \Gamma^t \to \Lambda$ describing the action of $e_\gamma$ on it, multiplicative modulo $n$ and matching scalars modulo $n$), and `hE4` (over an algebraically closed field $k$ over $\bar k$, every degree-$r^{d}$ self-isogeny pair of a curve pulled back from $A_0$ which preserves the level comes, up to powers of $r$, from some $e_\gamma$). Moreover an order $R_2 \leq R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) such that each element of $R_1$ lies in $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), and a family $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base subject to `hE5a` (additivity on points, commutation with the $\Lambda$-action, preservation of the level), `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3` (unit, contravariant multiplicativity, integers acting through $\Lambda$), `hE5c` (the conjugate composed with $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility of $\hat e$ on $r^{k}\gamma$ with $e_\gamma$ up to powers of $r$) and `hE5e` (the joint matrix/formal-coordinate dictionary of `hE2` realised by a single $g$ for both $e$ and $\hat e$).
--
--   *Special formal modules, the moduli package and the $\Omega$-comparison.* A ring map $\iota : \mathrm{Zp2}\,r \to Onr$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the induced structure map (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a family $\eta$ attaching to each rigidified object over a nilpotent test algebra a point of $MD$, with `hη` (three clauses: $\eta$ identifies admissible rigidified objects exactly when they are isomorphic, it is natural in the test algebra, and every point of $MD$ is, on a Zariski cover by localisations, of the form $\eta(t)$ for admissible $t$); an injective ring map $E_0$ of the centraliser of the action and of $\varpi$ on $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ in the precise sense of `hE₀` (there is $m$ with $r^{m}M_2(\mathcal{O})$ inside the image and $r^{m}$ times the image inside $M_2(\mathcal{O})$). A family $e_D$ comparing the $G$-points of $MD$ over noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to pairs consisting of an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $Onr \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$, subject to `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity for every such $B$), `hfstD` (the first component is the structural leg), `hGLD` (the action of $g \in GL_2(K_0)$ on $G$-points corresponds to the twisted action on pairs, twisting the leg by $\mathrm{vdet}(g)$ powers of $Fr$ and pulling the datum back along $g^{-1}$), `hPiD` and `hPiexD` ($\pi$-translates exist and correspond to twisting the leg by $Fr$ while keeping the datum). A ring map $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   *Atkin–Lehner data over the residue field.* A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ over $\bar k$ with maps $aw, aw'$ realising the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), maps $bw, bw'$ forming an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ preserving the level (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which asserts that for every injective $E_0'$ as above a single $g \in GL_2(K_0)$ simultaneously realises the dictionary for all $e_\gamma$ and matches the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ with $r^{k_w'}\,g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   *The rigidified-curve comparison $\Xi$.* A family $\Xi$ assigning to a rigidified fake elliptic curve over a noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, rigidified along a leg $\psi : Onr \to B$, a $G$-point of $MD$, subject to `hΞleg` (over connected $B$ the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $Fr^{k}$ of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (compatibility with base change of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves admitting the displayed comparison data, with two $r$-power exponents) and `hΞdef` (over connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent, and given a formal-module structure $(X,\theta)$ on the curve, there are $j \leq 1$ and an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$, transported from $\theta_0$ through $\kappa$ and $\beta_0$ at exponent $j$, such that $\Xi$ is the $G$-point with leg $Fr^{-j}\psi$ and underlying point $\eta(t)$). An element $g_0 \in GL_2(K_0)$ with `heqΓ` (translating a rigidification by $e_\gamma$ corresponds, over connected $B$, to the action of a power of the scalar $r$ times $g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g_0^{-1}$ on $\Xi$-points) and `heqW` (the analogue for an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ and the element $\bar w$, under the hypothesis that $\bar r$ is invertible in $B$).
--
--   *The point functor and $\theta$.* A noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $C$ with $\pi$ nilpotent (`hCπ`) and an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\chi_C : Onr \to C$; a functor $PR$ on commutative $C$-algebras; a point map $\mathrm{ptR}$ which, for every $C$-algebra $S$ that is also an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra compatibly and every leg $\psi_S : Onr \to S$ equal to $\chi_C$ followed by $C \to S$, sends a fake elliptic curve with full level $n$ over $S$ together with a rigidification of it along $\psi_S$ to a point of $PR(S)$. The hypotheses on this datum are `hR2` (compatibility with maps $\phi : S \to S'$ of $C$-algebras: corresponding curves, level structures and rigidifications are carried to corresponding points), `hR3s` (surjectivity: every point of $PR(S)$ is of the form $\mathrm{ptR}(u,\rho)$), `hR3i` (injectivity, assumed only when $S$ has no idempotents other than $0$ and $1$: equality of points yields an isomorphism of curves with full level, together with comparison maps between the two rigidifications agreeing after multiplication by two powers of $r$), and `hR1` (the converse implication: such an isomorphism and comparison data force the points to be equal). Finally a family $\theta_S : PR(S) \to \Omega(S)$ into the Deligne-datum functor $\Omega(K_0,\pi)$, defined for noetherian $S$ as above, with `hθnat` (naturality in $S$ over $C$) and `hθ` (the characterisation $\theta_S(\mathrm{ptR}(u,\rho)) =$ the pullback along $(g_0^{-1})^{-1}$ of the Deligne-datum component of $e_D(\Xi(u_1,\rho))$).
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses the conclusion is the following. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field which is a $C$-algebra and an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with compatible scalars, let $g \in G$ and let $j \in \{0,1\}$. Say that a point $x \in PR(k)$ *has a presentation of label $(g,j)$* if $\pi$ is nilpotent in $k$ and there are a leg $\psi_S : Onr \to k$ equal to $\chi_C$ followed by $C \to k$, a fake elliptic curve with full level $n$ over $k$, written $u = (u_1,u_2)$, and a rigidification $\rho$ of $u_1$ along $\psi_S$, such that: $\mathrm{ptR}(u,\rho) = x$; there is a full level-$n$ structure $P_n$ on $u_1$ which is the height-normalised transport of $P_0$ along $\rho$ in the sense of `Rigidification.IsNormLevelTransport Fr θ₀ κ β₀ Φ ι coord P₀ ρ Pn`, and the level structure $u_2$ is the push-forward of $P_n$ by the action of $\chi(g)$; and the leg of $\Xi(u_1,\rho)$ equals the twist $Fr^{-j}\psi_S$.
--
--   Then:
--
--   (i) (Injectivity on label-$(g,j)$ points.) For all $x, x' \in PR(k)$, if $x$ has a presentation of label $(g,j)$, $x'$ has a presentation of label $(g,j)$, and $\theta_k(x) = \theta_k(x')$, then $x = x'$.
--
--   (ii) (Surjectivity.) For every Deligne datum $d \in \Omega(K_0,\pi)(k)$ there is $x \in PR(k)$ which has a presentation of label $(g,j)$ and satisfies $\theta_k(x) = d$.
--
--   Thus, for each fixed $g$ and $j$, the family $\theta$ restricts to a bijection from the set of points of $PR(k)$ admitting a presentation of label $(g,j)$ onto $\Omega(K_0,\pi)(k)$.
--
--   This is the point-functor form of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison at algebraically closed points: after fixing a finite-level label $g$ and a parity $j$, the transcendental map $\theta$ to Deligne's functor of lines in lattices is a bijection on the corresponding locus of rigidified fake elliptic curves with full level $n$. It is stated for an abstract functor $PR$ on $C$-algebras rather than a representing scheme, and is used in the proofs of the existence and uniqueness statements for even rigidified pairs, which feed the relative representability of the rigidified moduli problem by the formal upper half plane.
